 using namespace bluetooth;
 
+/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * HFP software (in-band / over-HCI) SCO data path for the HF CLIENT role.
+ *
+ * WHY THIS EXISTS
+ * AOSP implements the software data path for the AG role only. bta/ag/bta_ag_sco.cc
+ * owns the HfpClientInterface endpoints, and stack/btm/btm_sco_hci.cc calls
+ * bta_ag_sco_read/write unconditionally. AAOS runs the HF role and not the AG --
+ * measured on the built image rather than taken from a parent makefile:
+ * bluetooth.profile.hfp.hf.enabled=true is in system/build.prop (from
+ * car_product/properties/bluetooth.prop:41 via car_system.mk's TARGET_SYSTEM_PROP),
+ * while bluetooth.profile.hfp.ag.enabled is UNSET and HeadsetService.java:290 reads
+ * it as BluetoothProperties.isProfileHfpAgEnabled().orElse(false), so AG is off by
+ * default. This product does NOT inherit
+ * device/google_car/common/post_google_car.mk, which is where an explicit
+ * hfp.ag.enabled=false would otherwise come from.
+ * So on this product the SCO PCM has nowhere to go: HeadsetClientStateMachine.java even says
+ * "Bluetooth SCO connection from the HF role is not entirely supported for routing
+ * and volume purposes". This block mirrors the AG implementation for HF.
+ *
+ * HOW IT IS GATED -- and why not the same way as AG
